WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:08.029
I love jumping into problems, when I find
that problem that I really care about,

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:14.228
and I really want to see through,
I can't really focus on anything else.

00:00:14.228 --> 00:00:16.882
Commitment is definitely
one way of phrasing it,

00:00:16.882 --> 00:00:19.230
I've phrased it as well as just obsession.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.518
And I'll continue to be obsessed with
it until the problem is solved, or

00:00:23.518 --> 00:00:27.752
I realize that I just for whatever
reason I'm incapable of solving it.

00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:31.861
I'm the son of two Hollywood
screenwriters, they write sitcoms,

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:33.674
so they're comedy writers.

00:00:33.674 --> 00:00:37.530
And growing up in a household where
we're always laughing and it was funny,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.530
it was amazing.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:43.386
They work together and they always work
for themselves and they were able to set

00:00:43.386 --> 00:00:48.332
the stage for their own career, and
I really inherited that heavily from them.

00:00:48.332 --> 00:00:52.632
When I was a lot younger, I liked playing
video games on the computer, but I wanted

00:00:52.632 --> 00:00:56.393
to be able to modify the video games or
maybe even make my own video game.

00:00:56.393 --> 00:01:00.029
And I realized the tool required
to do that was programming, so

00:01:00.029 --> 00:01:02.442
I was able to teach myself how to program.

00:01:02.442 --> 00:01:06.437
And seeing the advancements around
programming, especially with artificial

00:01:06.437 --> 00:01:10.607
intelligence, made me realize that there
were tremendous opportunities in a wide

00:01:10.607 --> 00:01:14.379
variety of industries where you could
apply these programming tool sets.

00:01:14.379 --> 00:01:18.663
[SOUND] There's really two themes in my
life that I think tie everything back

00:01:18.663 --> 00:01:22.413
together, one of those is,
human nature and human psychology.

00:01:22.413 --> 00:01:26.046
The other major theme is this idea of
learning the tools to create things,

00:01:26.046 --> 00:01:28.746
really revolve around technology and
programming and

00:01:28.746 --> 00:01:30.754
understanding new tech as they emerge.

00:01:30.754 --> 00:01:35.738
I was observing the crazy advancements
in artificial intelligence,

00:01:35.738 --> 00:01:38.111
deep learning computer vision.

00:01:38.111 --> 00:01:43.255
And realized that if we were to use
these extremely intelligent computer

00:01:43.255 --> 00:01:47.893
systems to automatically identify
things like guns and knives,

00:01:47.893 --> 00:01:52.721
we don't have to rely on human beings
staring at these images to do.

00:01:52.721 --> 00:01:56.613
Synapse technology provides
automated detection capabilities for

00:01:56.613 --> 00:02:01.106
X-ray security checkpoints, so
we automatically detect threats like guns,

00:02:01.106 --> 00:02:05.278
knives and ammunition, as opposed to
having to rely on human operators.

00:02:05.278 --> 00:02:06.954
[MUSIC]

00:02:06.954 --> 00:02:11.102
We ran a study just to give you a sense
of how improved the numbers are, and

00:02:11.102 --> 00:02:15.801
we gave somebody who's never seen one of
these X-ray security machines before.

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:18.476
And we compared that to
someone who's certified and

00:02:18.476 --> 00:02:20.373
trained on these X-rray machines.

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:23.621
The person who's never seen
this X-ray machine before,

00:02:23.621 --> 00:02:25.344
but was aided by our software,

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:29.931
was able to catch twice as many threats as
the person who was trained to catch them.

00:02:29.931 --> 00:02:34.761
If we can keep one more person from being
shot in one of these horrific acts of

00:02:34.761 --> 00:02:38.758
violence, to me that is
essentially mission accomplished.

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:42.408
&gt;&gt; Rather get pushed back-
&gt;&gt; I graduated MIT in three years and

00:02:42.408 --> 00:02:44.885
I knew that I wanted
some kind of fourth year,

00:02:44.885 --> 00:02:48.681
I found Stanford Business School and
I thought what could be better?

00:02:48.681 --> 00:02:53.571
It felt immediately relevant to what
I was working on at the company,

00:02:53.571 --> 00:02:58.886
being surrounded by lecturers and
professors and teachers and guess I felt

00:02:58.886 --> 00:03:04.732
like I had that love and support from some
of the classes and the experiences here.

00:03:04.732 --> 00:03:09.434
It suddenly made me realize just
how valuable this place is, and

00:03:09.434 --> 00:03:11.972
how much I was able to get out of it.

00:03:11.972 --> 00:03:14.711
I learned more during my MBA two year

00:03:14.711 --> 00:03:19.563
than I ever could have at my company
alone over a decade of work.

00:03:19.563 --> 00:03:22.355
My parents, from an early age,
always said,

00:03:22.355 --> 00:03:26.162
you can do anything you want in the world,
but be your own boss.

00:03:26.162 --> 00:03:29.354
And that's always really
resonated with me, and

00:03:29.354 --> 00:03:33.083
I've always followed my passions and
done exactly that.

00:03:33.083 --> 00:03:36.451
What gets me excited every day and
makes me want to wake up and

00:03:36.451 --> 00:03:40.531
go to work is feeling like I'm
solving genuine problems in the world.

00:03:40.531 --> 00:03:43.420
Whether that's in this case
making the world safer place or

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.503
whatever else it may be,
that's what motivates me every day.

00:03:46.503 --> 00:03:47.992
And that's what gets me excited and

00:03:47.992 --> 00:03:51.277
that's where I feel like there's just
tremendous opportunity in the world.

00:03:51.277 --> 00:03:54.002
[MUSIC]

